course: dictionary
lesson: 13819
title: Irrigate: bringing water on purpose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Irrigate is a verb, so it is something you do. It means to bring water to land, crops or a wound on purpose, using pipes, channels or sprinklers. The water is not an accident: someone is directing it to where it is needed.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «The farmer will irrigate the dry fields this summer.» Dry fields need water, and a farmer grows crops, so irrigate must mean bringing water to the fields so the crops can grow. Test your meaning in the sentence: "The farmer will water the dry fields" still makes sense, so you are close.
explain: Irrigate can also be a medical word. «Nurse Amira irrigated the cut on Tom's knee with clean water.» A cut needs cleaning, and the water is washing it out with a steady flow. That is the second meaning: to wash a wound or an eye with water.
fact: Irrigate means to bring water to land or crops on purpose, using pipes, channels or sprinklers.
fact: Irrigate can also mean to wash out a wound or an eye with a steady flow of water or liquid.
fact: Irrigate is a verb, so its forms are irrigates, irrigated and irrigating.
fact: «The ancient Egyptians irrigated their crops with water from the Nile.» They brought river water to their fields so the crops could grow.
fact: If something gets in your eye, you can irrigate it with clean water.
exercise: «The farmer will irrigate the dry fields this summer.» What does irrigate mean? | To sweep the fields clean | To cut down trees for wood | To dig a hole to bury seeds | *To bring water to land so crops can grow
exercise: «Nurse Amira irrigated the cut on Tom's knee with clean water.» What is the nurse doing? | Painting the cut with bright colours | *Washing the cut with a steady flow of water | Freezing the knee with ice | Bandaging the cut tightly
exercise: «The Egyptians irrigated their crops from the Nile.» Find and copy the word that shows this happened in the past. | *irrigated | crops | Nile | Egyptians
test: «Yesterday, the gardener ___ the vegetable patch.» Which form of irrigate fits the gap? | irrigates | irrigating | *irrigated | irrigate
test: Jayden says irrigate means water that arrives by accident, like from a burst pipe. Is he right? | *No, irrigate means water brought on purpose | Yes, a burst pipe is irrigation | Yes, it means water that spills everywhere | No, it only means washing clothes
exam: «The doctor used a steady flow of water to irrigate the child's eye.» What does irrigate mean here? | To take a photo of the eye | *To wash out with a steady flow of water | To rub the eye hard with a cloth | To cover the eye with a patch
